3 Splits and versioning
After cleaning, the pipeline:

builds the final features: the description joined with its complementary text fields, and the categorical variables;
splits the data into training, validation and test sets;
writes the splits to S3 in a folder named after the extraction date;
records this folder, along with the list of features, in a shared configuration file read by the training code.

Every training run logs its input datasets in MLflow. The run is therefore tied to an exact snapshot of the data.

5 Development workflow
The team works in an umbrella repository, codif-ape, which gathers all the repositories above as Git submodules that track the main branch. It is a workspace, not a release unit: each repository keeps its own version tags and its own CI.
All repositories follow the same conventions:

uv for Python environments;
pre-commit with ruff for code quality;
Renovate for dependency updates;
GitHub Actions for building images and documentation.

5 Why MLOps
A model trained once and never touched again does not stay good for long, for several reasons:

the way declarants describe their activity drifts over time, as new trades, new legal forms and new phrasings appear;
business experts report errors that must be fixed quickly and permanently;
the classification itself changes: the move from NAF rev. 2 to NAF 2025 required recoding the entire training set (see Data);
the libraries the model depends on get deprecated (fastText) or move to new major versions (MLflow, PyTorch).

The rest of this note describes the system that makes this work routine: reproducible training, a versioned model registry, an API deployed through GitOps, continuous monitoring and annotation, and a retraining protocol with explicit quality gates.

